; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Mouse Theremin
; CATEGORY: Music & Sound
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     mouse, buttonp, mousepos, pitch, exponent
; SUMMARY:  A theremin you play by holding the mouse button and sliding left and right; the pitch glides smoothly and the path is painted in color.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   A theremin is an electronic instrument played without touching
;   it: moving your hand closer raises the pitch. Here the mouse is
;   your hand. Hold the left button and slide: further right is
;   higher. Moving up the screen makes the notes shorter and choppier,
;   moving down makes them smooth. Your gestures are painted as a
;   trail whose color shows the pitch.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run, then hold down the left mouse button in the turtle
;   world and move around. Let go to stop the sound. Press Stop when
;   you are finished. Change :snap to "true to land only on the notes
;   of a scale.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * BUTTONP tells if a mouse button is held down right now
;   * a loop that checks the mouse many times a second (polling)
;   * pitch grows exponentially: each octave doubles the frequency
;   * ROUND to snap a smooth value to the nearest whole step
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Set :snap to "true and play a tune.
;   * Make the range three octaves by changing the 2 in PITCH.AT to 3.
;   * Make the trail wider when the notes are longer.
; ============================================================

make "snap "false
make "low 220          ; the pitch at the far left of the screen

; Turn an x position into a frequency. Across the 800 step width we
; climb 2 octaves; each octave is 12 half steps.
to pitch.at :x
  localmake "steps 24 * (:x + 400) / 800
  if :snap [make "steps round :steps]
  output :low * power 2 :steps / 12
end

; Higher up the screen means shorter, choppier notes.
to length.at :y
  output 30 + (300 - :y) / 6
end

; Hue from pitch: low notes are red, high notes blue.
to color.at :x
  localmake "k (:x + 400) / 800
  output (list 255 * (1 - :k) 80 255 * :k)
end

to draw.guides
  setpencolor [70 70 90]
  ; one faint line per octave
  foreach [-400 0 400] [penup setxy ? -300 pendown setxy ? 300]
  penup setxy -390 270 pendown setpencolor 7
  label [Hold the left button and slide: right is higher, up is choppier]
end

to play
  localmake "p mousepos
  if not buttonp [penup setpos :p wait 2 stop]
  setpencolor color.at first :p
  setpensize 6 pendown setpos :p
  tone pitch.at first :p length.at last :p
  sound list 0 15
end

clearscreen
hideturtle
setbackground [15 15 25]
setlabelheight 16
draw.guides
penup
print [Hold the left mouse button down in the turtle world and move.]
forever [play]
